1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,125
A data set is one table, file, index or API path that a

2
00:00:05,125 --> 00:00:10,468
connection reads, and it is what reports and data pipelines actually use.

3
00:00:10,468 --> 00:00:14,729
This video adds data sets from a file and from a web API,

4
00:00:14,729 --> 00:00:19,260
reviews the fields the site discovers, and previews the rows.

5
00:00:23,860 --> 00:00:26,587
A connection reaches a system.

6
00:00:26,587 --> 00:00:29,768
A data set picks one resource in it:

7
00:00:29,768 --> 00:00:34,010
a file, an API path, a table or view, or an index.

8
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:40,067
Every data set has a name, and a technical name that reports and pipelines store.

9
00:00:40,067 --> 00:00:43,300
The technical name can't change after you save.

10
00:00:44,050 --> 00:00:46,624
A data set is read live or imported.

11
00:00:46,624 --> 00:00:50,301
A live data set asks the system every time it is read.

12
00:00:50,301 --> 00:00:54,714
An imported one copies its rows into the site on a schedule, which

13
00:00:54,714 --> 00:00:56,430
the next video covers.

14
00:00:57,180 --> 00:01:00,171
The site also reads the data set's fields:

15
00:01:00,171 --> 00:01:04,222
their names, their types, and which of them identify a row.

16
00:01:04,222 --> 00:01:06,590
You review them before you save.

17
00:01:10,810 --> 00:01:15,047
Open Tools, Data Connectors, Data Sets, and click Add Data Set.

18
00:01:15,047 --> 00:01:17,134
Pick the connection to read.

19
00:01:17,134 --> 00:01:21,800
They are grouped by kind on the left, and Filter searches their names.

20
00:01:22,299 --> 00:01:24,166
Enter a Name, Customers.

21
00:01:24,166 --> 00:01:28,584
The technical name is made from the name when you leave it empty.

22
00:01:28,584 --> 00:01:33,190
In Resource, start typing and choose from what the connection lists:

23
00:01:33,190 --> 00:01:35,119
here, the customers file.

24
00:01:35,577 --> 00:01:40,523
Under Access, choose Live, so reports always read the file as it is now.

25
00:01:40,523 --> 00:01:43,566
The settings for files say how to read it:

26
00:01:43,566 --> 00:01:49,717
the delimiter, whether the first row names the columns, and for an Excel file, which sheet.

27
00:01:50,175 --> 00:01:51,929
Click Discover fields.

28
00:01:51,929 --> 00:01:57,130
The site reads the file and lists its columns, each with the type it guessed.

29
00:01:57,130 --> 00:02:01,390
The customer ID is an integer, the since column is a date, and

30
00:02:01,390 --> 00:02:05,275
the postal code stays text, so its leading zero is kept.

31
00:02:05,725 --> 00:02:10,871
Clear Show to hide a field from reports, as with the email address here.

32
00:02:10,871 --> 00:02:15,292
Change a Name or Display name, or a Type, for example to read

33
00:02:15,292 --> 00:02:16,809
text as a number.

34
00:02:16,809 --> 00:02:20,834
And tick Identifier on the field that identifies a row:

35
00:02:20,834 --> 00:02:22,285
the customer ID.

36
00:02:22,736 --> 00:02:27,632
Pick a Default date field if reports should filter by a date by default:

37
00:02:27,632 --> 00:02:28,385
Since.

38
00:02:28,385 --> 00:02:29,766
Then click Save.

39
00:02:33,966 --> 00:02:39,414
The list shows each data set, how it is read, and how many fields it has.

40
00:02:39,414 --> 00:02:42,586
Open Actions on a row and click Preview.

41
00:02:43,037 --> 00:02:47,989
The preview shows the first rows exactly as reports and pipelines read them.

42
00:02:47,989 --> 00:02:52,157
The email column is gone, and the postal codes keep their zero.

43
00:02:52,621 --> 00:02:54,704
Edit opens the data set again.

44
00:02:54,704 --> 00:03:00,083
The technical name is locked, because reports, pipelines and read permissions store it,

45
00:03:00,083 --> 00:03:03,843
and the page shows the permission that reads this data set.

46
00:03:03,843 --> 00:03:05,751
Everything else can change.

47
00:03:09,928 --> 00:03:12,640
Now a data set for the Sales API.

48
00:03:12,640 --> 00:03:17,369
For a web API, the resource is a path on the connection's base

49
00:03:17,369 --> 00:03:19,385
URL, here slash orders.

50
00:03:19,385 --> 00:03:22,028
Name it Orders, and choose Live.

51
00:03:22,480 --> 00:03:25,832
The settings below belong to the connector.

52
00:03:25,832 --> 00:03:30,416
The method is GET, or POST with a JSON body, and the response

53
00:03:30,416 --> 00:03:33,153
format is JSON, CSV or JSON Lines.

54
00:03:33,153 --> 00:03:37,532
The records path says where the rows are in a JSON answer:

55
00:03:37,532 --> 00:03:39,380
here, data dot items.

56
00:03:39,840 --> 00:03:43,356
Pages says how the API splits a long answer:

57
00:03:43,356 --> 00:03:48,981
by page number, by offset, with a cursor, or with a link to the next page.

58
00:03:48,981 --> 00:03:54,535
This one uses page numbers, and the page size is set to twenty five rows.

59
00:03:54,535 --> 00:03:57,770
Most pages stops a read that never ends.

60
00:03:58,221 --> 00:03:59,949
Click Discover fields.

61
00:03:59,949 --> 00:04:04,270
Tick Identifier on the order ID, pick the ordered on date as the

62
00:04:04,270 --> 00:04:06,431
default date field, and save.

63
00:04:09,832 --> 00:04:13,708
The preview shows orders from every page of the API.

64
00:04:13,708 --> 00:04:17,852
A live data set asks the API again each time it is read.

65
00:04:22,040 --> 00:04:27,535
A SQL Server data set reads a table or a view, or one custom query instead:

66
00:04:27,535 --> 00:04:31,741
a single select statement, such as a join of two tables.

67
00:04:31,741 --> 00:04:34,930
Query timeout limits how long it may run.

68
00:04:35,382 --> 00:04:39,671
An Elasticsearch data set reads an index, an alias or a pattern.

69
00:04:39,671 --> 00:04:44,266
The query picks the documents to read, and the scroll keep alive says

70
00:04:44,266 --> 00:04:47,882
how long the cluster holds a read open between pages.

71
00:04:54,856 --> 00:05:00,206
Both can also be imported on a schedule, with the refresh settings of the next video.

72
00:05:02,206 --> 00:05:06,974
A data set picks one resource of a connection, gets its fields reviewed,

73
00:05:06,974 --> 00:05:10,031
and is previewed exactly as reports read it.

74
00:05:10,031 --> 00:05:13,026
Next, keep an imported data set up to date.

75
00:05:13,026 --> 00:05:14,616
Thanks for watching.

